\ Codegen narrows a parameter from the freeze-time call-site proof, with no type
\ annotation written anywhere in this file. Running the AOT binary checks the
\ narrowing is semantics-preserving; the sibling stats test proves it removes the
\ interpreter fallbacks.
\
\ Results are printed rather than asserted through `testing`. The inference pass
\ walks the top-level body with an abstract stack, and the first call it cannot
\ model abandons that stack, leaving every later call site unobserved. A
\ `test-section` call is one of those, so an assertion-based file would suppress
\ the narrowing it is trying to check.

scale: ( x -- y ) [ 2 * ] ;
fhalve: ( x -- y ) [ 0.5 * ] ;

\ Self-tail-recursive: the arithmetic lives in the inlined false branch, so the
\ narrowing must survive the branch and re-read the parameters each iteration as
\ the back-edge rewrites their slots. Float, because a fixnum sum is provable only
\ under the lock -- fixnum arithmetic can promote to bignum, so the pass refuses to
\ type its result unless the build rejects the promoting path.
fsum-to: ( acc n -- r ) [
  dup 0.0 = [ drop ] [ swap over + swap 1.0 - fsum-to ] if
] ;

\ Call sites disagree on this parameter's type, so the proof fails and it stays
\ opaque. Both shapes must still work.
twice: ( x -- y ) [ dup + ] ;

\ Only `a` is proved here, so codegen narrows neither parameter: a pair with one
\ narrowed and one opaque side would take the concrete path, whose tag check bails
\ on the opaque side, and an AOT bail aborts rather than resuming interpreted. Both
\ shapes must keep working, for `+` and for `<`.
add2: ( a b -- r ) [ + ] ;
below2?: ( a b -- r ) [ < ] ;

7 scale .
3.0 fhalve .
0.0 100.0 fsum-to .
0.0 0.0 fsum-to .
5 twice .
2.5 twice .
1 2 add2 .
1 2.5 add2 .
1 2 below2? .
1 0.5 below2? .
